#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106897} :activity/id #uuid "f9fd061e-b62c-59b3-95e7-cb70aa81601f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049254 :topic/id #uuid "e6f9ad03-abe7-52d6-8a6a-acbcb8fbeef7" :topic/math-academy-id 1351} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106897 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107154 :tutorial/content "Consider the triangle below with vertices $(0,0),$ $(2,1)$ and $(2,3).$\n\n![](images/f7/f7c16deb083b467c063c900196274d08c532ad4633354f375530cb2cbe270491.png)\n\nWhat is the image of this triangle under the action of the transformation  $\\mathbf{T}$ represented by the matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the image of the triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all vertices of our triangle written as columns:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are $(0,0),$ $(2,0),$ $(2,0).$ Notice that these points lie on the same line. Two of them even coincide!\n\nSo, the image of our triangle under the action of $\\mathbf T$ is the following line segment:\n\n![](images/88/88383b8eca1805c158f80d998d844d4c666b15d118a0ec26b4a1ba43c781fc76.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\nThis is an example of a linear transformation that does not preserve linear independence. \n\n* First, notice that since [math]\\det(T)= 0,[/math] the matrix [math]T[/math] is singular, which means that the vectors represented by the columns of [math] T,[/math] given below, are *not* linearly independent. \n\\[\n\\left\\{ \\begin{bmatrix} 1 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix}, \\:  \\begin{bmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{bmatrix} \\right\\}\n\\]  \n\n- Recall that for any linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ and region $D,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(\\mathbf{T}(D)) = |\\det(T)| \\cdot \\text{Area}(D).\n$$\n\n \nSince $|\\det(T)|  = 0$ in this case, any object applied to $\\mathbf T$ collapses to an object with zero area.\n\n- Also, the fact that $|\\det(T)|  = 0$ tells us that $\\mathbf T$ has no inverse transformation. Therefore, it is impossible to recover the original triangle knowing only its image and the matrix $T.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "260cef8c-3f93-55cc-ac8c-258b2555991e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7693 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "91fe4680-2271-5bd9-a679-84fcae2b774c" :step/math-academy-id 44641 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106898}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106898 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105893 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105460 :question/id #uuid "dcd503a8-a9c6-524b-a8ac-0641ce3e1107" :question/math-academy-id "e-12423"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca93cd65-65e3-521e-a1e3-2721f45fb6a6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049420}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275183} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360722}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Coordinates of an Image Formed by a Singular Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "1608691b-a3f7-54f4-82a1-b60b84369109" :step/math-academy-id 44642 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105894 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105461 :question/id #uuid "964efb4a-6e6b-5fc1-a993-20aa2b0acdaa" :question/math-academy-id "e-12424"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d1252e9-f2ac-5adf-9cf5-ef48e35fb747" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275176} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275177} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360745} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388890} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388897} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395215}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Image of an Object Formed by a Singular Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "cdca196d-b54e-5e0f-8148-f89ac24552f6" :step/math-academy-id 44645 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106901}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106900 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105895 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105462 :question/id #uuid "4aa087bf-ee6c-5e90-9431-c4be6b207333" :question/math-academy-id "e-12438"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fea43b6-fd7b-5edc-b6b7-7f8b2cbab502" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049044}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106068} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281406} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281407} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281408} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281410} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281411} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281413}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Type of Image Formed by a Linear Transformation"} :step/id #uuid "fe3d3d75-3555-5f42-96ec-0bc13603e728" :step/math-academy-id 44693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106901 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107155 :tutorial/content "Given a linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with an associated $2\\times 2$ matrix $T,$ there are three possibilities regarding the type of object formed under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ by a two-dimensional preimage. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the three-sided polygon $\\mathcal P$ with vertices $(0,1)$, $(2,0)$ and $(2,1),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/97/97689c7aaf7f6d42314fd01cb36b557df8081880659bcea08905e6c57f358ac1.png)\n\nLet's describe the three possible types of object formed by the image of $\\mathcal P{:}$\n\n- Case where $T$ is non-singular:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is non-singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $\\mathcal P$ and then compute the product $TX{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is a triangle.\n\n![](images/14/145753e4f69dfa67bb45f59b7e9ed8bca789c589d4c5340ce2112363fa70cd7b.png)\n\n- Case where $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular and has at least one nonzero entry:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation with the following matrix representation:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe can easily check that $T$ is singular by computing its determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-6\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of  $\\mathbf{T}$ is a line segment.\n\n![](images/46/46d387dbf6678d324135dbbb3439688c0bdbf9f9567baef56f34aa4aa9ee22e3.png)\n\n- Case where $T$ is the zero matrix:\n\nLet $\\mathbf T$ be the linear transformation represented by the zero matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nClearly, $T$ is singular. Now, computing the image of $\\mathcal P$ under the action of $\\mathbf T$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the image's vertices are all $(0,0)$, the origin $O$ of the coordinate system. \n\n![](images/db/dbb9fce92da1f231a31e110f6cabb6faaf1bb43961a6c1de012c7c302c7af928.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "0bc95d9b-74ad-509c-8c28-e4e34606b376" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7702 :tutorial/title "Image Types Formed by Linear Transformations"} :step/id #uuid "c766a184-7dd4-51fc-8815-53eaecbc38c4" :step/math-academy-id 44695 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106902}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106902 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107156 :tutorial/content "In general, for any linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with associated $2 \\times 2$ matrix $T,$ we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is non-singular, i.e., $\\det(T) \\neq 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $\\mathbf{T}$ is singular, i.e., $\\det(T) = 0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $T$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $T$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is the point $(0,0),$ the origin of the coordinate system." :tutorial/id #uuid "593c9d77-3990-5aef-8f2f-1ef87dd41c59" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7708 :tutorial/title "A Summary of Image Types"} :step/id #uuid "b9b4bf14-bd02-5562-869e-62d28d1f3854" :step/math-academy-id 44720 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106900}}] :activity/title "Singular Linear Transformations in the Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105986}]}
